ere the eye travels next, usually from the main image to the headline…"},{"title":"Choose colour and type people can actually read","skill":"Accessible design","idea":"Choose a colour mood that fits the audience, such as warm and hopeful, then check that text stands out clearly from its background. Dark text on a light background, or the reverse, is usually safer than two mid-tones."},{"title":"Turn three numbers into one honest chart story","skill":"Data storytelling","idea":"Start with the finding, not the chart: write the headline as one plain sentence about what changed. Pick the chart type that shows that change simply, such as a line or bars for change over time."},{"title":"Plan a five-slide carousel with one idea each","skill":"Carousel structure","idea":"A carousel works like a short story: a hook that stops the scroll, a build that adds interest, proof that the claim is believable, and an invitation that tells people what to do."},{"title":"Review an AI image before you use it","skill":"Image critique","idea":"Review every AI image before using it, with a short checklist. Look for odd hands, faces or objects, text that is misspelt or meaningless, a style that does not match your brand, stereotypes or missing groups, and questions about…"},{"title":"Pitch an idea in three persuasive slides","skill":"Pitch storytelling","idea":"A short pitch works best in this order: problem, insight, ask. The problem makes the audience feel the need, the insight shows what you noticed that others missed, and the ask says exactly what you want them to approve."},{"title":"Storyboard a five-frame campaign in words","skill":"Campaign storyboard","idea":"A campaign storyboard connects everything you have practised: one audience, one feeling, one action, a clear order of frames and a visual detail that repeats."}]},{"id":"marketing","name":"AI Digital Marketing","topics":[{"title":"Turn a customer problem into a clear offer","skill":"Customer insight","idea":"A useful offer connects one specific customer, one urgent problem, and one believable outcome. Use AI to explore variations, then remove claims you cannot prove. The customer should know what to do next."},{"title":"Write a campaign brief an AI can actually use","skill":"AI briefing","idea":"Specify the audience, problem, evidence, tone, constraints and output format. Ask for alternatives, then judge them against a rubric. Never ask the model to invent customer results."},{"title":"Design a follow-up that earns a reply","skill":"Lead conversion","idea":"Reduce the next step. Answer a likely practical question, provide one helpful detail, and ask one easy question. Respect opt-outs and WhatsApp messaging rules."},{"title":"Ask customers fair questions, not leading ones","skill":"Customer research","idea":"A leading question hints at the answer you hope to hear, so people agree to be polite. A yes/no question gives you one word and no reasons."},{"title":"Test one change at a time in an A/B test","skill":"Message testing","idea":"An A/B test compares two versions that differ in exactly one thing, such as the headline or the call to action. If you change several things, you cannot tell which change caused the result."},{"title":"Choose one channel and justify the choice","skill":"Channel choice","idea":"Pick a channel by asking three questions. Where does this audience already spend time and pay attention? What does it cost in money and effort for you? Can you measure what it brings? A channel that fits the audience but cannot…"},{"title":"Find the number that deserves your effort first","skill":"Measuring results","idea":"Not every number tells you the same thing. Clicks show interest, conversations show contact, and qualified enquiries show people who might really buy. To find cost per qualified enquiry, divide total spend by qualified enquiries."},{"title":"Plan two weeks of content you can really keep up","skill":"Content planning","idea":"A content plan starts with one theme, such as “gifts made by hand”, so every post supports the same message. Mix formats, for example a photo, a short reel, a story and a message to existing customers."},{"title":"Spot a risky ad claim and rewrite it honestly","skill":"Responsible marketing","idea":"Responsible marketing means you can support every claim and treat people fairly. Watch for guarantees you cannot control, fake urgency like “only 3 seats left” when seats are not limited, and testimonials that are invented or…"},{"title":"Capstone: build a one-page campaign plan","skill":"Campaign planning","idea":"A strong campaign plan fits on one page and links its parts together. It names the customer and their problem, a simple offer, one channel, one test, one success metric and one risk."}]},{"id":"development","name":"AI Full-Stack Development","topics":[{"title":"Turn a vague idea into a testable feature","skill":"Product thinking","idea":"Start with the smallest complete user action. Define required inputs, success, failure and what must be private. Ask AI for an implementation only after the behaviour is explicit."},{"title":"Review AI-generated code before it ships","skill":"Code review","idea":"Treat browser input as untrusted. Derive identity from a verified server session and prices from a trusted catalogue. Validate inputs and test negative cases, including another user’s record."},{"title":"Choose the records and fields for a booking system","skill":"Data modelling","idea":"A data model lists the things your app remembers (records) and the facts about each one (fields). Give every record a unique identifier, and decide which field must never repeat, such as one seat in one batch for one student."},{"title":"Define one endpoint before asking AI to build it","skill":"API contract","idea":"An API contract describes one endpoint in words: its action, the inputs it needs, what it returns on success and what it returns on failure."},{"title":"Decide which checks run in the browser and server","skill":"Input validation","idea":"Browser checks give fast, friendly feedback, such as a missing field or a phone number that is too short. They can be bypassed, so they are only a convenience."},{"title":"Debug with AI: find the cause, not just a patch","skill":"Debugging with AI","idea":"Debugging means narrowing down where the problem happens. First reproduce it, then check each stage in order: what the browser sent, whether the server received it, and whether the data was stored."},{"title":"Who you are versus what you may do","skill":"Access control","idea":"Authentication answers who you are, usually by logging in. Authorisation answers what you are allowed to do once the system knows who you are. A logged-in user is not allowed everything."},{"title":"Write three test cases for one booking rule","skill":"Writing tests","idea":"A test case states a situation, the action taken, and the exact result you expect. Cover three kinds: a normal case that should work, a boundary case at the exact limit, and a failure case that should be refused."},{"title":"Plan a safe change with branches and rollback","skill":"Version control","idea":"Version control records every change so you can compare, review and undo it. Work on a separate branch, so the live version stays untouched until the change is ready."},{"title":"Capstone: a pre-launch checklist for the booking app","skill":"Launch readiness","idea":"A pre-launch checklist turns worry into specific questions across five areas: security, tests, privacy, error handling and monitoring."}]}];
